WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.320
There is a vending machine

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.920
for every 23 people in Japan.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.580
That's the highest vending machine per capita on the planet.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.860
After the business card fiasco I started

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:26.610
to become keenly aware of all the vending machines

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.430
that I saw here in Japan.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:31.320
I noticed:  they are everywhere!

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.360
Indeed, what we're looking at here is a Japanese institution.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.260
Behind me sits an entire shop

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.160
dedicated to chopsticks.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.620
Yes, I'm about to go inside.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.040
The first thing you have to know

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.900
in order to understand the vending machines,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.100
is that Japan is an aging country.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.320
The average age here is 46 years old,

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.220
which is almost double the world average.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.240
And the fertility rate is 1.4

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.660
which means the population is actually shrinking.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.200
This is actually a looming crisis

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.100
for Japan generally, but

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.000
one of the effects of it

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.400
is that the labor market is very expensive.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.680
There's a scarcity of low-skilled labor.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.540
So, instead of paying a sales clerk

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.220
to sit and collect your money when

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.840
you buy a piece of gum,

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.300
they just put it in a machine and automate the whole thing.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.060
And the same goes for real estate.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.600
Japan is one of the densest countries in the world.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.620
93 percent of the population lives in cities.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.440
People literally live in

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.280
apartment smaller than your SUV.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.960
So instead of paying a lot of money for a

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.480
store front, retailers will just slip a

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.350
little machine into an alleyway to save

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.940
a lot of money and they can still turn a

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.960
really good profit.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.980
According to one essay that I read from a Japanese

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.480
economist here in Tokyo, the bigger

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.700
explanation for the vending machines is

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.190
a fascination or even an obsession with

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.140
automation and robotics.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.380
Everything that can be automated here,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.060
is automated. When I go into order like a

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.060
ramen or breakfast,

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.880
more often than not i

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.980
order on a machine and I give a little

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.140
ticket to someone.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.340
It's indicative of a broader cultural trend of wanting to

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.860
automate every system you possibly can.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.480
Every taxi in Tokyo has automated doors

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.640
that the driver controls.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.019
I don't want to overstate this. There's still a

00:02:58.019 --> 00:03:01.170
major appreciation for handcrafted

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.380
artisanal goods here in Japan.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.360
A good example of this is the seven-year-old

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.859
coffee shop I just got out of, where they

00:03:07.859 --> 00:03:10.409
literally use a weighted scale to weigh

00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:12.299
their coffee beans before grinding them

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.540
and brewing them to order

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.180
To cool down their coffee they put it

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.330
into a metal vessel and spin it around a

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.320
giant ice cube.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.520
So yes, they love automation but they're still very much

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.560
in touch with the handmade.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.700
So another thing that totally contributes is this: coinage.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.960
So much coinage.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.019
The one big caveat to the whole automation thing is that

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.580
they haven't really gotten on board with credit cards yet.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:57.440
Everything is cash based.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.380
And because of that you always have coinage.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:02.140
One of their highest coin is worth like five dollars

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:03.880
and let's be honest:

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.890
there's nothing more satisfying than unloading

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.780
some of the change in your pocket into a

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.520
vending machine for some yummy treat.

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:13.520
My personal favorite item is hot green tea

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:18.340
comes out wonderfully warm and you just

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.960
wonder how you got so lucky.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.840
So Japan is an aging nation with expensive labor

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.300
and a love for robots and too many coins

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.080
in its pocket

